=== Step 3: instr.c msh command read-back behavior (source-level, confirmed before boot) ===
Read applications/instr.c directly (lines ~588-800):
  - `instr_interval` with argc<2 prints current g_instr_interval_ms AND the derived tick count
    AND RT_TICK_PER_SECOND, i.e. it DOES support a true read-back of current value, not just
    a usage string. Confirmed by source, not yet confirmed live (QEMU never reached — see blocker).
  - `instr_tier` with argc<2 similarly prints current tier name + INSTR_WALK_SEGMENT_LISTS compile
    flag. True read-back supported.
  - `instr_trace [stride]` prints "INSTR_TRACE_BEGIN run_id=... written=<total> retained_from=<start>
    stride=..." — `written` vs `retained_from` is exactly the overflow/wrap indicator the plan asked
    for: if written > INSTR_TRACE_SLOTS (8192), retained_from = written - 8192 (wrapped), else 0.
  - INSTR_TRACE_SLOTS = 8192u (confirmed by source, #define at line 119), matches the plan's
    assumption. At 20 ms / 60 s requested duration, expected sample count is on the order of
    ~3000 (60000/20 = 3000 ticks of the periodic soft timer, modulo any missed deadlines) which
    is well under the 8192-slot capacity — no wrap expected. NOT YET OBSERVED LIVE.
  - `instr_dump`'s JSON includes `"run_id"` (echoes g_accum.run_id, set by the most recent
    instr_reset call) — this is the run_id-mismatch gate's data source: compare requested run_id
    (argument to instr_reset) against the dump's own run_id field. Not yet exercised live.
  - mech_app_limited / mech_unsent_pending / mech_ack_pending fields are emitted unconditionally
    in instr_dump's JSON (only populated meaningfully under tier=full per the plan's own text).

