=== BLOCKER — tap0 network interface does not exist and cannot be created non-interactively ===

`tc qdisc show dev tap0` → "Cannot find device \"tap0\"" (checked before touching anything else).
`ip link show` / `ip addr show` / `ip tuntap show` → no tap0, no tap interface of any kind present.

Attempted the QEMU boot command (07_reproduction_notes.md's documented, "VERIFIED" invocation,
inside a freshly created detached tmux session `pilot20`, exactly as the task instructed):

  tmux new-session -d -s pilot20 -x 220 -y 50 \
    "qemu-system-arm -M vexpress-a9 -kernel rtthread.elf -nographic \
     -drive file=sd.bin,format=raw,if=sd -audio none \
     -net nic -net tap,ifname=tap0,script=no,downscript=no"

The tmux session died immediately (tmux ls -> "no server running" seconds later). Re-ran the exact
same command in the foreground with `timeout 5` to see the real stderr:

  qemu-system-arm: -net tap,ifname=tap0,script=no,downscript=no: could not configure
  /dev/net/tun (tap0): Operation not permitted

/dev/net/tun is world-rw (crw-rw-rw- root:root), so the failure is not a /dev/net/tun permission
problem — creating/configuring a NEW tap interface (TUNSETIFF/TUNSETPERSIST-equivalent via netdev
ioctls) requires CAP_NET_ADMIN, which this unprivileged user does not have. `getcap` on
qemu-system-arm itself returns nothing (no file capabilities set). No persistent tap device exists
(`ip tuntap show` empty) and no setuid qemu-bridge-helper path is configured
(/etc/qemu/bridge.conf absent).

Root cause located in ~/.bash_history (read-only inspection, not executed): tap0 was previously
brought up by hand, OUTSIDE any tracked script, via plain (interactive-password) sudo:

  sudo ip tuntap add dev tap0 mode tap user $USER
  sudo ip link set tap0 up
  sudo ip addr add 192.168.10.1/24 dev tap0

This sequence requires full interactive sudo (confirmed: `sudo -n ip tuntap add dev tap0 mode tap
user vboxuser` -> "sudo: a password is required", exit 1). It is NOT covered by the one approved
non-interactive mechanism (`sudo -n /usr/local/sbin/tfg-set-netem <ms>`), which only runs
`tc qdisc del|replace dev tap0 ...` on an ALREADY-EXISTING tap0 device — it does not and cannot
create the interface itself. The task instructions explicitly forbid plain `sudo tc`/other
interactive-sudo invocations in a non-interactive session (they will hang). Creating tap0 requires
the same category of privileged, interactive-only operation; I did not attempt it, did not try any
password, and did not look for or use any workaround (no setuid binary exists to exploit, and
using one would not be appropriate in any case).

This blocks ALL 24 attempts under the current host network state, not just attempt 1 — it is an
environment/infrastructure precondition, not something specific to the 20ms pilot's own settings.
No measurement, no QEMU boot reaching msh, no iperf, and no netem change (tfg-set-netem itself
would also report "Cannot find device" against tap0, even though it is the approved tool, since
there is nothing for it to operate on) can proceed until tap0 exists again on this host.

